blem is that  it went in the wrong direction last time  you have to work with other people and i  suppose we're not going to get let it go  in the wrong direction this time i am  absolutely determined trevor the prime  minister is absolutely determined not to  let it happen again  brian o'toole who was the treasury  official charged with  essentially leading on the  devising sanctions regime after 2014  uh said to me in the interview we're  going to run later in this program that  actually you have to understand there  are limits to what sanctions can do  they could they knew that they couldn't  get putin out of crimea in 2014 with  sanctions what they hoped to do and what  he thought they did was to delay further  aggressions what is our real objectives  with sanctions here he's not going to  bit back out of ukraine because of  sanctions is he  the real objective is to degrade the  russian economy  because what we know is that putin's war  machine is funded by revenues from oil  and gas so what we have to do is reduce  dependency  on oil and gas we have to cut the  ability  of russia to raise money on the  international financial markets we're  cutting the technology that they can use  in their military complex they've got  very advanced weapons we're going to  stop them accessing western technology  so over a period of time it's harder and  harder for putin to fund his army harder  and harder for him to create that new  technology and harder harder and harder  for him to wage war but i completely  agree with your analysis that this will  take time all right this is why i'm  saying this is a lot going to be a  long-running conflict the ukrainians are  and will resist it's very very hard for  russia to gain permanent control over  ukraine when the public of ukraine do  not want that and that is what this is  why we warn putin that this would be a  long-running quagmire but the role of  sanctions is degrading the russian  economy over time all right let's talk  about a couple of things that we can do  um  when again we spoke you said that there  would be nowhere to hide for putin's  associates the oligarchs now roman  abramovich has relinquished control of  chelsea football club but he still owns  it  and that means that if you come after  his assets for an example if you were to  come after his assets  chelsea football club would be beyond  your reach in truth  unless you change the way that all  companies operate in london the  oligarchs and their british lawyers will  still be able to protect their assets  won't they  well this week we'll be introducing the  economic crime bill which will create a  lot more transparency  around the structures of these companies  and make it much clearer uh what is  going on i've compiled a hit list of  oligarchs  people who is abramovic on it i'm not  going to say who is on the list but what  i can tell you is there are over 100  billionaires in russia  we have compiled a hit list of oligarchs  we're working through and putting the  cases together and every few weeks we  will sanction new oligarchs there will  be a rolling program of sanctions we  will go through the hit list we're  targeting oligarchs private jets we'll  be targeting their properties uh we'll  be targeting uh other possessions that  they have and there will be nowhere to  hide  all right look um  lastly everyone knows newton's law to  every action there's a reaction we have  we'll do the sanctions and so on  it's estimated that uh energy bills  might now climb to three thousand we can  expect inflation above eight percent  ukraine produces grain price of bread  will rise so there will be a cost to all  of this  for us  i wonder um  for us to make a difference  our people are going to have to  face some suffering  some consequences  are you going to level with the british  people that in order to force change in  moscow  people in manchester and maidstern  bertha tidfield anywhere else begins  with m are going to make sacrifices in  this country is for what neville  chamberlain once described as a quarrel  in a faraway country between people of  which we know  nothing  are you going to be clear with britain  that if we're going to  fight this long battle they are going to  have to sacrifice  well first of all the sacrifices that we  are going to have to undertake in  britain are nothing like the sacrifices  that people in ukraine are suffering now  they are having to arm themselves they  are having to fight on the streets  due to a completely unprovoked invasion  by the russians  and my thoughts are with the people of  ukraine and the difficult struggle that  they they they have ahead of them  but we're not just fighting for the  people of ukraine  and the sovereignty and  self-determination of ukraine  this  long-running  conflict is about freedom and democracy  in europe  because if we don't stop putin in  ukraine  we are going to see others under threat  the baltics  poland moldova  and it could end up in a conflict with  nato we do not want to go there that is  why it's so important  that we make the sacrifices now and yes  there will be an economic cost here in  britain there will be a cost in terms of  access to oil and gas markets we don't  import much here but  there are  there are a lot of um  imports into  a continental europe from russian gas so  inevitably there will be impacts but we  have to  and i i firmly believe the british  public  understand the price we would pay if we  don't stand up to peter now  the price we would pay is much greater  in the long term and frankly  over the past 15 years we haven't done  enough to stand up to putin we're now  facing the reality of what that means  and that is why we are taking a much  tougher stand now  liz trust thank you very much thank you
 

